WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:05.600
Hey again. In this second video, I will talk about how I used AI

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.240
to build this. So, first of all,

3
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.560
it all starts with this repository I have that's called Muthur, from the

4
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.920
Alien spaceship, which I use for all of my repositories,

5
00:00:16.363 --> 00:00:19.723
and which contains the execution loop and the skills,

6
00:00:19.883 --> 00:00:22.843
the rule sets, everything that you need to build with agents,

7
00:00:23.003 --> 00:00:25.083
everything that I need to build with agents,

8
00:00:25.643 --> 00:00:27.883
regardless of the stack that you use.

9
00:00:28.523 --> 00:00:33.125
Initially, I used it to make a clone, or a so-called spin-off,

10
00:00:33.445 --> 00:00:35.765
of my own professional website.

11
00:00:36.085 --> 00:00:40.645
And when I say spin-off, it means that it copied not just its design

12
00:00:40.645 --> 00:00:43.685
system but, more importantly, its coding discipline,

13
00:00:44.005 --> 00:00:47.605
which means the very strict linter rules,

14
00:00:48.288 --> 00:00:52.928
the Feature-Sliced Design approach, and a lot of additional checks

15
00:00:52.928 --> 00:00:57.408
that I added along the way as I was developing my website, and that

16
00:00:57.408 --> 00:01:01.168
I also use in other apps of mine.

17
00:01:01.488 --> 00:01:06.011
Then I used the brief that I had for the assessment to come

18
00:01:06.011 --> 00:01:09.211
up with a plan, which is pretty high level,

19
00:01:09.451 --> 00:01:14.251
and from there I went to implementing it with Claude

20
00:01:14.331 --> 00:01:18.331
Code. Usually, I would go about it in a way where I would split

21
00:01:18.331 --> 00:01:22.613
it into a lot of pieces and then implement them one by one,

22
00:01:22.933 --> 00:01:27.333
each via its own pull request. But here I went in a different direction.

23
00:01:27.973 --> 00:01:32.613
I made it all on one feature branch and

24
00:01:33.013 --> 00:01:35.973
one pull request, because of the time constraints

25
00:01:36.336 --> 00:01:39.776
and the nature of the task. What's important is that, despite this,

26
00:01:40.016 --> 00:01:43.376
it was never overflowing the context of the LLM,

27
00:01:43.696 --> 00:01:48.496
because I have this approach, from the skills that I use from this Muthur

28
00:01:48.656 --> 00:01:52.096
repo, which is to have a plan inside

29
00:01:52.379 --> 00:01:56.939
the repo. And whenever one agent finishes its small piece of work, which is

30
00:01:56.939 --> 00:02:00.139
within, let's say, 200,000 tokens,

31
00:02:00.459 --> 00:02:04.059
it will pause the plan. Here's what it actually looks like.

32
00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:08.139
So it puts the plan on pause, and then I can start a new

33
00:02:08.902 --> 00:02:11.462
session with this plan,

34
00:02:11.622 --> 00:02:14.502
just saying "go", and it will go do the next chunk, and so on

35
00:02:14.582 --> 00:02:19.142
and so on. And so from there, in about a hundred commits with

36
00:02:19.142 --> 00:02:22.822
two hundred and fifty files, we got to a stage where we had an app

37
00:02:22.902 --> 00:02:27.984
that was actually working. And it was pretty well structured

38
00:02:27.984 --> 00:02:31.584
from the get-go. First of all, because we had these forks during which I

39
00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:33.904
checked that it looked as I wanted it to look,

40
00:02:34.064 --> 00:02:39.024
and secondly, because the discipline that I already mentioned was already baked

41
00:02:39.024 --> 00:02:42.027
in. Then I went and put a code review,

42
00:02:42.107 --> 00:02:46.987
and each item of this code review, the agent included in a separate GitHub

43
00:02:46.987 --> 00:02:51.787
issue, also using a skill that I have, because GitHub is a first-class

44
00:02:51.787 --> 00:02:54.107
citizen in all of my work. So,

45
00:02:54.187 --> 00:02:58.790
what it did, it created an issue which included all of these

46
00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:02.950
notes that I found, and it structured them into pieces.

47
00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:06.070
I then prioritized which pieces I wanted most,

48
00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:09.990
for example, to make it look more presentable from the get-go.

49
00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:14.992
I implemented them. And an interesting further piece of work I

50
00:03:14.992 --> 00:03:18.432
did was implementing the upload of PDFs,

51
00:03:18.592 --> 00:03:22.272
which I already showed in the first video.

52
00:03:22.832 --> 00:03:25.872
So, interestingly, it all went in one shot.

53
00:03:26.032 --> 00:03:28.272
I didn't have to change pretty much anything.

54
00:03:28.715 --> 00:03:33.435
Here's how it looked inside the Claude Code web interface

55
00:03:33.435 --> 00:03:35.915
itself. It's nice.

56
00:03:36.875 --> 00:03:43.435
But there are some things you can't go about in one shot, because

57
00:03:43.995 --> 00:03:47.798
an agent will often think too technically.

58
00:03:48.118 --> 00:03:51.958
And so, for example, there was this point in the README where it said

59
00:03:51.958 --> 00:03:55.958
what I would do with more time, and it went for technical choices,

60
00:03:56.278 --> 00:04:00.518
whereas I myself went for more strategic, big-picture choices.

61
00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.520
I'm running out of time, so just to finish it:

62
00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.640
I even made the whole set of talking points for this video

63
00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.880
and the first video with AI as well.

64
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.600
And finally, because Harish mentioned he likes using AI for internal tools,

65
00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.763
I have this hook that writes down every cost

66
00:04:21.083 --> 00:04:25.323
spent during the work, based on API rates.

67
00:04:25.483 --> 00:04:27.563
And when you go to the repo itself,

68
00:04:27.643 --> 00:04:31.083
you can run pnpm costs, and it will show you a nice

69
00:04:31.083 --> 00:04:35.126
breakdown of which work took how many dollars.

70
00:04:35.286 --> 00:04:38.646
Or, let's say so, imaginary dollars, because these are API rates,

71
00:04:38.726 --> 00:04:41.286
not what you actually spend with the subscription. Phew,

72
00:04:41.446 --> 00:04:43.606
so I have fifteen seconds left.

73
00:04:43.926 --> 00:04:47.206
It was too short. I hope to discuss it in more detail with you

